From 9d2e708e77bca502270de8049243cb8924cf0678 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:46:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [DVBridge] An HD video's level 5 offsets read as its own
 where its pictures say so

The level 5 offsets of an RPU are counted in the samples of the picture
that it describes (ETSI GS CCM 001 V1.1.1, 6.2.2). A picture smaller
than 3840x2160 may also carry the RPU of a 3840x2160 master that it was
scaled or cropped from, and nothing in the metadata says which, so
dvbridge_dv_margins() takes the smaller margin of the two readings on
each side. For an HD stream with an RPU of its own (a 1080p 2.39:1 film,
offsets 138) that sent the display half of the bars, and the zoom to the
active area took away only half of them.

The pictures tell the two apart. Native output already measures a few
pictures against their offsets (dvbridge_l5check.h); it now also
measures the band between the two readings where a picture has one: it
is black on every side where it is bars, with picture next to it, and
has picture across it where it is picture. Four pictures over three
seconds that find it bars, and none that finds it picture, make the
video's offsets read as offsets of its picture (dvbridge_dv::l5_picture:
of the video, for every value of its offsets, and kept through a
restart, as what the offsets are counted in is the RPU's); one picture
across the band takes that back for the rest of the video, so the
smaller reading stays wherever there is doubt. Until it is decided, a
picture a second is measured in the first minute of the video; once it
is, one at each cut, at most every second, and one every five seconds.

The serializer, the zoom (dvbridge_l5_area(), with
CDVBridgeGLES::L5OfPicture()) and the picture area of the player
information read the offsets the one same way. The player information
read them as offsets of the picture alone, which gave twice the bars of
an HD encode that kept its master's RPU, and fewer rows than it has to a
crop of exactly the bars. A 3840x2160 picture is untouched: its two
readings are one, and nothing more is measured for it.

The measurement now takes the offsets and their bars from the frame's
metadata, in samples of the picture, so that it also runs for a picture
drawn through a view (the zoom draws through one, and such a picture was
never measured: the zoom could take away picture for the RPU of another
framing of the film), and goes on where the offsets were left aside, so
that they are taken again as dvbridge_l5check.h has always said.
